\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The host's own suite, and the plans it ships, after the extension.} \textbf  {(A)}~The pre-registered falsification condition. The host's suite comprises sixteen checks; sixteen pass and none fails, marked by the cross at zero. The suite is invoked as a subprocess and its own summary file is read, so the figure plots the host's report rather than our restatement of it. Had this bar been non-zero the integration would have failed and the failure would have been the result. \textbf  {(B)}~The negative control, by fixture world. All eleven shipped plans that contain no ladder execute, across all three of the host's worlds. The world is chosen by the host's own selector; an earlier version of this control loaded a single world and reported three failures that were properties of the control rather than of the extension. \textbf  {(C)}~Requests issued by each shipped plan. The plans still reach their sources and still spend budget: the control is not passing because nothing happens. \textbf  {(D)}~Verdicts across the shipped plans, by step. The surface is concentrated on \textsf  {answer} with a ridge at \textsf  {starved}, which is the distribution the host's suite was written to produce and which the extension leaves in place.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel1}{{1}{4}{\textbf {The host's own suite, and the plans it ships, after the extension.} \textbf {(A)}~The pre-registered falsification condition. The host's suite comprises sixteen checks; sixteen pass and none fails, marked by the cross at zero. The suite is invoked as a subprocess and its own summary file is read, so the figure plots the host's report rather than our restatement of it. Had this bar been non-zero the integration would have failed and the failure would have been the result. \textbf {(B)}~The negative control, by fixture world. All eleven shipped plans that contain no ladder execute, across all three of the host's worlds. The world is chosen by the host's own selector; an earlier version of this control loaded a single world and reported three failures that were properties of the control rather than of the extension. \textbf {(C)}~Requests issued by each shipped plan. The plans still reach their sources and still spend budget: the control is not passing because nothing happens. \textbf {(D)}~Verdicts across the shipped plans, by step. The surface is concentrated on \textsf {answer} with a ridge at \textsf {starved}, which is the distribution the host's suite was written to produce and which the extension leaves in place}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel1@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][3][]4}{}{}{}}
