\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The ladder occupies a cost tier the host already had.} \textbf  {(A)}~Budget spent per step in a plan with two source accesses, one set operation and one ladder. The two source steps spend one request each; the union and the ladder spend nothing. The ladder's bar is not merely small, it is zero. \textbf  {(B)}~Allocated against spent. Every non-source step is allocated the nominal unit the host's water-filling allocator assigns to all-or-nothing steps and then spends none of it. That asymmetry is the host's existing conservatism, visible here on the union step as much as on the ladder, and the ladder adopts it rather than introducing a third regime. \textbf  {(C)}~Cumulative requests along the plan. The trace rises at each source step and is flat across the union and the ladder, so adding ladders to a plan cannot make it overrun its budget. \textbf  {(D)}~Requests spent over the number of source steps and the number of ladder steps. The surface is a plane rising in one axis and constant in the other: cost depends on source accesses alone.}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel2}{{2}{6}{\textbf {The ladder occupies a cost tier the host already had.} \textbf {(A)}~Budget spent per step in a plan with two source accesses, one set operation and one ladder. The two source steps spend one request each; the union and the ladder spend nothing. The ladder's bar is not merely small, it is zero. \textbf {(B)}~Allocated against spent. Every non-source step is allocated the nominal unit the host's water-filling allocator assigns to all-or-nothing steps and then spends none of it. That asymmetry is the host's existing conservatism, visible here on the union step as much as on the ladder, and the ladder adopts it rather than introducing a third regime. \textbf {(C)}~Cumulative requests along the plan. The trace rises at each source step and is flat across the union and the ladder, so adding ladders to a plan cannot make it overrun its budget. \textbf {(D)}~Requests spent over the number of source steps and the number of ladder steps. The surface is a plane rising in one axis and constant in the other: cost depends on source accesses alone}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel2@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][5][]6}{}{}{}}
