\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The frozen corpus: two public services with different schemas.} \textbf  {(A)}~Sizes and coverage. Four hundred KEGG enzyme records, of which $372$ carry at least one reaction; $904$ Reactome reactions, of which $353$ carry at least one catalyst. The catalyst count was zero on the first extraction and the discrepancy was found by querying the live service rather than by accepting a plausible zero. \textbf  {(B)}~Reactions per pathway, ranked. The corpus supplies chains of genuinely differing length, the longest containing $58$ reactions, which is what makes the chain-length axis of panel~6 a property of data rather than of a parameter we chose. \textbf  {(C)}~Reactions per enzyme record in KEGG, on a logarithmic count axis. The distribution is heavy-tailed: most records name few reactions and a few name many. \textbf  {(D)}~Inputs, outputs and catalysts per Reactome reaction. The cloud sits mostly in the plane of zero catalysts, with a populated slab above it, and the two axes of participants are correlated but not equal---the schema-level difference from KEGG, which records substrates and products against an EC number instead.}}{9}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel5}{{5}{9}{\textbf {The frozen corpus: two public services with different schemas.} \textbf {(A)}~Sizes and coverage. Four hundred KEGG enzyme records, of which $372$ carry at least one reaction; $904$ Reactome reactions, of which $353$ carry at least one catalyst. The catalyst count was zero on the first extraction and the discrepancy was found by querying the live service rather than by accepting a plausible zero. \textbf {(B)}~Reactions per pathway, ranked. The corpus supplies chains of genuinely differing length, the longest containing $58$ reactions, which is what makes the chain-length axis of panel~6 a property of data rather than of a parameter we chose. \textbf {(C)}~Reactions per enzyme record in KEGG, on a logarithmic count axis. The distribution is heavy-tailed: most records name few reactions and a few name many. \textbf {(D)}~Inputs, outputs and catalysts per Reactome reaction. The cloud sits mostly in the plane of zero catalysts, with a populated slab above it, and the two axes of participants are correlated but not equal---the schema-level difference from KEGG, which records substrates and products against an EC number instead}{figure.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel5@cref}{{[figure][5][]5}{[1][8][]9}{}{}{}}
