\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Composition read against the corpus.} \textbf  {(A)}~Composite power against the number of rungs at three uniform rung powers, with vertical lines at the six longest pathway lengths in the corpus. At a rung power of $0.05$ even the longest chain in the corpus falls short of unity, which is the saturation result placed against real chain lengths rather than against an arbitrary axis. \textbf  {(B)}~Rungs required to reach three targets, as a function of rung power, on a logarithmic axis. The count diverges as the rung power falls, and the three curves separate sharply as the target approaches unity. \textbf  {(C)}~Sensitivity against rung power for a single ladder of corpus length. Points lie exactly on $P/(1-\pi _j)$, which increases in $\pi _j$: control lies at the strongest rung, not the weakest. \textbf  {(D)}~The saturation surface: composite power over chain length and rung power. It rises to unity in the far corner and stays low along the low-power edge however long the chain, which is the statement that a long sequence of weak steps does not substitute for a short sequence of strong ones.}}{10}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel6}{{6}{10}{\textbf {Composition read against the corpus.} \textbf {(A)}~Composite power against the number of rungs at three uniform rung powers, with vertical lines at the six longest pathway lengths in the corpus. At a rung power of $0.05$ even the longest chain in the corpus falls short of unity, which is the saturation result placed against real chain lengths rather than against an arbitrary axis. \textbf {(B)}~Rungs required to reach three targets, as a function of rung power, on a logarithmic axis. The count diverges as the rung power falls, and the three curves separate sharply as the target approaches unity. \textbf {(C)}~Sensitivity against rung power for a single ladder of corpus length. Points lie exactly on $P/(1-\pi _j)$, which increases in $\pi _j$: control lies at the strongest rung, not the weakest. \textbf {(D)}~The saturation surface: composite power over chain length and rung power. It rises to unity in the far corner and stays low along the low-power edge however long the chain, which is the statement that a long sequence of weak steps does not substitute for a short sequence of strong ones}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel6@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][9][]10}{}{}{}}
